Hi [~accountid:6052891960d39e006f72f0f0], [~accountid:5ab2387a8fb3642a6fd2a1ce],

Please find below in green the answers to the questions posed by the Bolt Food Business Team:



*BL: Our business team usually renegotiates commissions and other clauses of the contracts signed with the partners, after the beginning of their term. What would be the best way to reflect the renegotiated clauses? Is it possible to do it through a confirmation email sent to the partner by someone with powers to do so within Bolt organization (i.e. the company manager) or is it necessary to do it through an addendum?*

{color:#36b37e}The keystone of the theory of contracts is the of freedom of contract, which consists of the power that the parties have, within the limits of the law, to fix, according to their will, the content of the contracts they enter into, to enter into contracts other than those provided for in the Code or to include in them any clauses they wish - Article 405(1) of the Civil Code.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Contractual freedom is only recognised, in the words of Article 405(1), "within the limits of the law".{color}

{color:#36b37e}The principle of private autonomy, which is a fundamental idea of our civil-law system, has as its privileged means of action the legal transaction. It is also clear that private autonomy is subject to various limitations, from the outset the formation of the contract must be made within the limits of the law (Article 405 of the Civil Code), and it is not dispicable to recall Articles 280 and 294 of the same Code, among others.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Once the "limits of the law" are observed in the "modelling" of contracts, the contract becomes an act with mandatory force: once concluded, the contract, fully valid and effective, constitutes imperative law between the parties - Article 406(1) do Civil Code.{color}

{color:#36b37e}This principle of stability of contracts is subject to deviations: the contract may be modified or terminated at the will of the parties or by legislative provision.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Therefore, {color}{color:#36b37e}*the best way to reflect the renegotiated clauses will be through the new agreement entered into between the parties*{color}{color:#36b37e}, which in accordance with the provisions of Article 219 of the Civil Code will not depend on a special form, except when the law requires it.{color}

{color:#36b37e}The general rule of freedom of form applies both to typical and non-typical contracts, since no reason or legal requirement imposes otherwise, although the non-typical ones may raise some issues.{color}

{color:#36b37e}In conclusion, the best way to reflect the renegotiated clauses {color}{color:#36b37e}+*is through an addendum.*+{color}

*BL: Some of our competitors have contracts in force with a term of 2 years, notice periods of 180 days to terminate such contracts and penalties in the amount of 10% of the contract fees for failure to comply with the notice periods.* *What is the local regulation on the duration of the contracts and the notice periods to terminate contracts? Are clauses like the one described above legal or, on the contrary, could they violate competition law in Portugal?*

{color:#36b37e}In Portugal, there is no specific legislation for so-called delivery partners of digital platforms.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Article 405(1) of the CC sets out the following rule: individuals, in contracts, may act of their own free will. The limits imposed by law constitute the exception.{color}

{color:#36b37e}The closest contract to the one entered is a service contract, Article 1154 of the Civil Code.{color}

{color:#36b37e}To atypical service contracts are applicable, under the terms of the provisions of Article 1156 of the Civil Code, with the necessary adaptations, the provisions that govern the mandate.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Thus, the said contract may be freely revoked by the parties, Article 1170.{color}

{color:#36b37e}However, under the terms of Article 1172, the party that revokes the contract for the provision of services, to which the rules of the mandate are applicable, must compensate the other party for the loss that the latter suffers if : {color}

{color:#36b37e}_a) If it has been so agreed;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_b) If irrevocability has been stipulated or the right of revocation has been renounced;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_c) If the revocation comes from the principal and concerns an onerous mandate, whenever the mandate has been conferred for a certain period of time or for a certain matter, or if the principal revokes it without the convenient prior notice;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_d) If the revocation comes from the mandator and has not been made with the convenient prior notice._{color}

{color:#36b37e}Thus, {color}{color:#36b37e}*the stipulation of a clause as described above shall be lawful provided it cannot be deemed manifestly excessive*{color}{color:#36b37e}, pursuant to article 812(1) of the Civil Code.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Such clause embodies a true penalty clause (compensatory), by means of which the contracting parties establish in advance the amount of compensation that must be paid (by the principal) in the event of early termination of the contract by the appellant (Articles 810 and 811 of the Civil Code).{color}

*BL: Under Portuguese law, are exclusivity clauses where partners agree to work exclusively with a certain platform for a certain number of years or months legal?*

{color:#36b37e}To contracts entered into between companies, since under the terms of article 2 of the Competition Law Regime (RJC), "all economic activities exercised, whether permanently or occasionally, in the private, public and cooperative sectors" are covered, and under the terms of article 3 of the RJC "company, for the purposes of this law, \[is] any entity exercising an economic activity consisting of the offer of goods or services in a given market, regardless of its legal status and method of financing".{color}

{color:#36b37e}Now, Article 9 (1) of the RJC states that:{color}

{color:#36b37e}_"1 - Agreements between undertakings, concerted practices between undertakings and decisions by associations of undertakings which have as their object or effect the prevention, distortion or appreciable restriction of competition in the whole or part of the national market shall be prohibited, those which consist of:_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_(a) fixing, directly or indirectly, purchase or selling prices or any other trading conditions;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_(b) limit or control production, distribution, technical development, or investment;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_(c) share markets or sources of supply;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_(d) apply dissimilar conditions to equivalent transactions with other trading parties, thereby placing them at a competitive disadvantage;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_(e) making the execution of contracts subject to acceptance by the other parties of supplementary obligations which, by their nature or according to commercial usage, have no connection with the subject of such contracts._{color}

{color:#36b37e}_f) Establish, within the scope of the supply of goods or accommodation services in tourist resorts or local accommodation establishments, that the other party or any other entity cannot offer, in an electronic platform or in an establishment in physical space, prices or other sale conditions of the same good or service that are more advantageous than those practiced by an intermediary, who acts through an electronic platform.”_{color}

{color:#36b37e}Paragraph 2 of this same article sanctions that {color}{color:#36b37e}_"Except in cases where they are deemed justified_{color}{color:#36b37e},{color}{color:#36b37e} _in accordance with the following article, agreements between undertakings and decisions by associations of undertakings prohibited by the preceding paragraph shall be null and void."_{color}

{color:#36b37e}Notwithstanding all this, these agreements may be considered justified if, in accordance with article 10 of the RJC, the companies demonstrate that the agreement contributed to improve the production or distribution of goods or services or to promote technical or economic development provided that, cumulatively{color}

{color:#36b37e}(i) They reserve to the users of these goods or services an equitable share of the resulting benefit;{color}

{color:#36b37e}(ii) do not impose on the undertakings concerned restrictions which are dispensable to the attainment of these objectives{color}

{color:#36b37e}(iii) afford such undertakings the possibility of eliminating competition in a substantial part of the market for the goods or services in question{color}

{color:#36b37e}It is incumbent on the undertakings or associations of undertakings invoking the benefit of justification to prove the fulfilment of the conditions foreseen in the preceding paragraph.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Agreements between undertakings, concerted practices between undertakings and decisions by associations of undertakings prohibited by the previous article that, although not affecting trade between member states, fulfil the other requirements for the application of a regulation adopted under the provisions of article 101, paragraph 3, of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, shall be considered justified.{color}

*BL: Could you review the attached Agreement and give us your opinion on its legality under Portuguese law and whether it is advisable for us to use it to engage with partners?*

{color:#36b37e}After reviewing the attached contract, the only clause that raises doubts is clause 5 of the special conditions, because, as a rule, a stipulation of a clause that imposes a penalty clause for breach of the non-competition obligation, after termination of the contract, will be null and void, as it is contrary to the right to economic freedom, which includes the freedoms of work and initiative referred to in Articles 47(1), 58(1) and 61(1) of the Constitution of the Portuguese Republic.{color}

{color:#36b37e}In the contract for the supply of services, such as the one agreed, the relationship of trust assumes an important role, as it is a typical contract of cooperation between persons.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Trust requires the protection, in the period after the termination of the contract, of the expectations caused at the conclusion of the contract and in its fulfilment by the behaviour of the intervening parties, raising the question of the post-effectiveness of the accessory duties of conduct .{color}

{color:#36b37e}Within the scope of post-contractual effectiveness, protection must have different and lesser limits than those that existed until then. Once the contractual relationship ends, the parties recover the freedom which was limited by the contract to which they were bound and by the accessory duties of conduct arising therefrom.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Non-compete clauses inserted in contracts cannot serve as an instrument that allows to restrict, distort, or impede competition. {color}{color:#36b37e}*The ultimate objective of the non-compete obligation is the protection of the contracting party legitimate interests*{color}{color:#36b37e}, preventing the other party from using information collected and learned within the previous relationship and applying it in his new work or business relationship, benefiting the new contractor to the detriment of his former employer, or taking advantage of the elements to start a self-employed activity, thus competing with the former contractor.{color}

{color:#36b37e}The law does not prevent the parties, by agreement, from stipulating the non-compete obligation, although due to the nature of the interests, since it implies the limitation of a fundamental right, there must be {color}{color:#36b37e}*limits*{color}{color:#36b37e} to the possibility of this types of agreements.{color}

{color:#36b37e}However, and considering that fundamental rights are not absolute and that non-competition agreements after the termination of the contract are admitted in several similar contracts and that in our case the trust relationship assumes an important relevance, it seems to us that the conditions are met so that such an agreement may be considered valid, {color}{color:#36b37e}+*as long as it is in writing, contains a compensation for the one who assumes the no compete clause and does not exceed a period of two years.*+{color}

{color:#36b37e}It may, however, last for three years if the one that assumed the obligation in question has held positions of "{color}{color:#36b37e}_special relation of trust or has access to particularly sensitive information in terms of competition_{color}{color:#36b37e}". This period must be expressly provided for, under penalty of the nullity of the agreement or of the non-compete clause.{color}

{color:#36b37e}As it implies a restriction to the fundamental right, it is not all the activities that may be limited, {color}{color:#36b37e}*the limitation imposed shall have to present some relation with the activity whose performance may cause injury to the contractor*{color}{color:#36b37e}, respecting the principle of proportionality.{color}

{color:#36b37e}It is for the person invoking the non-competition clause in his favour to demonstrate that the knowledge acquired by the person who infringed it involves specific risks for the undertaking.{color}

{color:#36b37e}In the absence of an interest on the part of the employer justified by the risk of differential competition, the clause is void.{color}

{color:#36b37e}To overcome the difficulty of proof and quantification of the damages suffered (anticipated liquidation of those damages), a penalty clause may be established in favour of the principal, there being, however, the possibility of its reduction by the court according to equity when it is manifestly excessive (Article 812, paragraph 1, of the Civil Code).{color}

{color:#36b37e}Therefore, we believe that the period of 5 years provided for in the said clause should be reduced to two years.{color}

{color:#36b37e}These clauses should not be confused with the prohibition of unfair competition.{color}

{color:#36b37e}There is a general duty to refrain from the practice of conducts that constitute unfair competition, under the terms set out in Article 311 of the Industrial Property Code.{color}

{color:#36b37e}The article 311.º of the Industrial Property Code states:{color}

{color:#36b37e}_"1 - Constitutes unfair competition any act of competition contrary to the norms and honest practices of any branch of economic activity, namely_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_a) Acts susceptible of creating confusion with the company, establishment, products or services of competitors, whatever the means employed;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_b) False statements made in the exercise of an economic activity with the purpose of discrediting competitors;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_c) The unauthorized invocations or references made with the purpose of benefiting from the credit or reputation of a name, establishment or mark of others;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_d) False indications of own credit or reputation, regarding the capital or financial situation of the company or establishment, the nature or scope of its activities and business and the quality or quantity of the clients;_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_e) False descriptions or indications as to the nature, quality or usefulness of the products or services, as well as false indications as to provenance, locality, region or territory, factory, workshop, property or establishment, by any means whatsoever_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_f) The suppression, concealment, or alteration, by the vendor or any intermediary, of the denomination of origin or geographical indication of the products or of the registered mark of the producer or manufacturer in products intended for sale and which have not undergone any change in their packaging._{color}

{color:#36b37e}_2 - The measures laid down in Article 345 are applicable, with the necessary adaptations"._{color}

{color:#36b37e}The focus of unfair competition is to ensure that, in the market, economic agents - when exercising and intending to increase their activity - behave loyally and in accordance with the dictates of good faith, thus protecting, prima facie, competitors and, secondarily, consumers. {color}

{color:#36b37e}Unlike post pactum finitum non-compete obligations, the prohibition of unfair competition is a legal imperative.{color}

 

*BL: When negotiating exclusivity with partners, is there anything that we should take into account in the communications that we maintain with partners, i.e. any wording or concepts that we should not use to avoid going against competition law or another in Portugal?*

{color:#36b37e}There are no language restrictions, provided that the rules mentioned above are complied with.{color}



Should have any further questions, please do let me know.

