Further advice received from DLA 29 June:

*** 

* We understand that there is in PT (i) a prohibition for platform operators to own or finance or be an interested party in the acquisition, renting or leasing of TVDE vehicles and (ii) a banking monopoly principle.
* Considering that the upfront payment would come from Bolt Operations OU, it seems that our best option would be to enter into *a co-investment contract with the FO* and to *include a settlement arrangement in both contracts* (in the co-investment contract with the FO and in the financing agreement between the Bank and the FO) - would you agree with that? 

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} Yes. For the sake of clarity, by Bank you mean Car Dealer, correct?{color}

* I note that you also mentioned adopting a more general contractual object (for example, that the investment is aimed at a general corporate purpose). However we would *prefer to pay the bank directly*, if possible - or at least make it crystal clear that the funds should go towards buying the TVDE vehicles - so not sure how we can include a general contractual object in the contract…

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} Please refer to our answer below regarding the settlement arrangement in the financing agreement between the Bank and the FO.{color}

* We would also *like the* +*co-investment contract*+ *to cover the following*:
** 2 years exclusivity for each car that we ‘co-finance’ → By that, we mean that we ask them that each car spends X online hours per week on the Bolt platform.

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} Ok, we can include.{color}

* KPIs for the FO:
** 1 global KPI: 45,000€ GMV minimum per car after 18 months after 1st ride of each car, which corresponds to the 2,250€ (5% of advanced cash-back) we are paying - we can include a 2 months grace period (so total 18+2)
** KPIs/metrics to be tracked weekly/monthly (e.g. Minimum Online Hours (i.e. hours connected with that car on the Bolt Platform), Minimum acceptance rate (how many rides they accept with the car), Minimum average trips per week, Minimum GMV per week/month
** minimum 50 additional cars each (objective is 170 fo Apcar/ 150 for Wisentheimer).
** we need to guarantee that XS will remain open for at least 4 years with mutual exclusivity in case utilisation lowers in other categories.
* Cash-back (on top of the initial upfront payment): If the FO exceeds the GMV target over 18 months, Bolt gives the FO a 5% Cash-back on the excess performance (= bonus paid by Bolt to the FO).
* If they don’t reach the GMV target, the FO needs to reimburse us for the difference (= contractual penalty) → Bolt deduct it from the weekly compensations (possibly in installments).
* Regular check points to verify that they’re meeting the KPIs/ are on track for targets. 

→ So if we enter into such co-investment contract, covering the above, *how likely is it that we avoid the requalification as ‘co-financing contract’ and as ‘professional financing agreement’ or - on the other hand - that we get caught by one of these prohibitions*?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} There should be no risk of violating the banking monopoly principle because Bolt acts as an investment partner and not as a financing party. We understand that this is more an investment agreement than a financing agreement due to the characteristics of the proposed structure which are much closer to an investment agreement, but additionally we advise that “co-financing” is changed to “co-investment” because visually it helps deter any assumptions that this is a financing agreement. From a ride-hailing regulatory perspective, as we have previously stated, without prejudice to some risk that this may fall in the prohibition, we believe there are good grounds to understand that this is not the case.{color}

→ We’d like to understand better +what would be the+ +*risks of such set-up*+. In particular, *if we get caught* (for requalification as ‘co-financing contract’ or as ‘professional financing agreement'), *what are the consequences?* Fine? No criminal liability? Could we lose our license?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} From a banking regulatory perspective, the violation of the principle of banking monopoly is deemed an especially serious infraction punishable with fines ranging from €10,000 to €5,000,000. From a ride-hailing regulatory perspective, should Bolt be caught by the prohibition of financing or being a party to a contract for the acquisition, rental, leasing or use of TVDE vehicles, it incurs the risk of an administrative penalty of up to €15,000. In addition, and depending on the seriousness of the infringement, an accessory sanction of interdiction of the activity for a maximum period of 2 years can be applied. There is also a general provision in the TVDE legal regime that allows the IMT to suspend, limit or terminate the activity of a platform operator in case it does not comply at all times with the legal provisions regarding the exercise of the activity and the provision of its services. Therefore, we cannot exclude the risk for Bolt to be subject to these measures, including to be prevented from exercising the activity.{color}

→ Also, you mentioned that “_there is no_ +_return on capital_+” but what about the KPIs mentioned above and the fact that we would expect them to meet a certain GMV to make profit? Are we good on the ‘no return on capital’ requirement?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} This is not a typical financing, because there is no interest on the principal amount lent to the FO, thus no direct return on capital lent. Bolt will not earn an interest from the principal amount lent, but, instead, will have an indirect return through growth of sales and brand and marketing returns.{color}

* Regarding the +*settlement arrangement*+ *in the financing agreement between the Bank and the FO*, how would we make sure that this is included? Would there be an undertaking from the FO in the co-investment contract to include such provision in their contract with the bank? Bolt wouldn’t enter into a contract with the Bank? Could we ask to see a copy of the contract between bank and FO? Basically, how do we get comfort that this is included in the contract between FO and Bank?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:* {color}{color:#4c9aff}Bolt’s position should be safeguarded as follows:{color}

* {color:#4c9aff}A settlement clause in the contract to be entered into between Bolt and the FO states that Bolt will fulfil its payment obligation by transferring the relevant funds to an entity to be indicated by the FO. {color}{color:#4c9aff}*Should the FO comply*{color}{color:#4c9aff} with the interest of the parties in this agreement and indicate a reputable Car Dealer, the risk of the FO misusing the funds will be eliminated.{color}
* {color:#4c9aff}*Should the FO wrongfully indicate the transferee*{color}{color:#4c9aff}, the risk of misuse of the funds is mitigated by an obligation of the FO to indicate an entity consistent with the FO’s obligation of meeting the KPIs and which is satisfactory to Bolt. If the entity indicated by the FO is not consistent with the FO’s obligation to meet the KPIs and/or is not satisfactory to Bolt, this would give Bolt cause for termination.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}For the sake of clarity, by Bank you mean Car Dealer, correct?{color}

* +In the settlement arrangement+, in both contracts, could we add some *limitation of liability* → to limit Bolt’s liability to the amount we give and to say that we cannot be seen as a third party who could have to repay the loan in case the FO defaults?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:* {color}{color:#4c9aff}Yes, this could be included, except for intent or gross negligence.{color}

* As originally envisaged, *we could give the upfront funds directly to the car owner* (or the car dealer) but it seems to us that it might then be even more obvious that we are financing the cars + more risky for us (in terms of controlling the use of the funds) - so this would be our last resort option. I assume that if we put the funds in a bank account of the FO at the Bank specifically set up for that, and we have the settlement arrangement provisions, it is less risky for Bolt?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper Lisbon:* {color}{color:#4c9aff}We understand that settlement arrangements in both contracts ensure that the funds are directly transferred to Car Dealer. But if we are overlooking something in the structure, maybe we should have a quick call on this topic.{color}

+Remarks+:

* Regarding your statement “_In the proposed agreement, we understand there is an actual purchase of cars and an actual hiring of such cars to operate under the Bolt platform._“ → No, the FO buys the cars and they just promise Bolt exclusivity over these cars that we ‘co-financed’?

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:* {color}{color:#4c9aff}Understood.{color}

* We would like to pout the money in an FO bank account dedicated at the Bank - as and when the FO confirms that the cars have arrived at the car dealer and are available for him to buy. We wouldn’t get involved at the stage of the order from between the FO and the car dealer. So if one week, the FO confirms that 10 Fiat Pandas are available for him to buy, we put in the FO special bank account 10x EUR 2250 so the bank can use this money before wiring the full amount requested by the FO. The following month, it could be 20x EUR 2250.

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} It would be helpful to have a quick call on this as we understand that settlement arrangements in both contracts ensure that the funds are directly transferred to Car Dealer.{color}

* FYI - The 45,000€ GMV target comes from a calculation based on what a car brings in terms of GMV over 18 months (broadly speaking a car makes EUR 15 / hours, x35 online hours (of exclusivity) / week x 4weeks = broadly EUR 45K over 18 months per car).

{color:#4c9aff}*DLA Piper:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} OK thank you{color}

